From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: a draw whose pipeline is still building waits at most a per-frame budget, then is skipped (after zortos293/Lichii #465 "Bounded"), off by default

Switch: EDEN_UP_BOUNDED_BUILD_WAIT_MS=<1-100> (milliseconds a frame; a live switch, Id
BoundedBuildWait, not armed). Unset, 0 or anything else (a sign, blanks, trailing text, more
than 100): off. Suggested first value: 8 (Lichii #465's default).

What: on the PS5 the asynchronous shaders setting is off. So a draw whose driver build is not done
is recorded anyway, and the scheduler worker waits for the build before it goes on
(GraphicsPipeline::ConfigureImpl, "worker: waiting for a pipeline build"). The frame cannot finish
until the build does: on a first sight that is the hard freeze. With the switch on, in
PipelineCache::BuiltPipeline, a Switch 2 draw that may be skipped (CanSkipDraw: depth in use, or
more than a full screen quad, and not kept by EDEN_UP_ASYNC_DRAW_WAITS) waits on the GPU thread for
its build, within what is left of this frame's budget (GraphicsPipeline::WaitBuiltFor, which asks
the builder for it first when EDEN_UP_FAST_CFG is on). Built in time: drawn as before. Not built:
the draw is skipped this frame, the build goes on, and the game's next frame draws it again. Once
the frame's budget is spent, every draw with an unbuilt pipeline in that frame is skipped at once.
Draws that may not be skipped (a full screen quad without depth, or kept by
EDEN_UP_ASYNC_DRAW_WAITS) wait as before.

How it compares with what we have:
- 0540 EDEN_UP_ASYNC_DRAW_WAITS (rdenno 7a940ca) is the opposite: it makes MORE draws wait (Switch 2
  draws without depth), so font glyphs are never skipped. It has no budget. It does not do #465.
- 0508 EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION already has a per-frame budget (EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_WAIT_MS,
  default 2 per pipeline, and _FRAME_MS, default 4 a frame), but only for the shader TRANSLATION on
  worker threads. Once a translated pipeline is adopted, its driver build is waited for in full by
  the scheduler worker (0508's own comment says so). This patch bounds that second wait. The two
  budgets are separate.

Also in this patch: the three build_condvar.notify_one() calls in vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp become
notify_all(). The scheduler worker and (with this switch) the GPU thread can now wait on the same
pipeline at once; notify_one could wake only one of them and leave the worker waiting for ever.
With one waiter, notify_all does the same as before.

Where it comes from (credit): zortos293/Lichii PR #465 by Mario Matheus Pombal (MarioMatheusPombal),
"native_async_draws = Bounded" with "native_pipeline_wait_ms" (default 8): PipelineBuild::WaitFor,
DecideNativePending, a budget reset per frame, and the "Bounded pipeline wait" log line. His
numbers (RX 6700 XT, Ocarina, first visit to the village): draws held for unbuilt pipelines in the
worst 10 s went from 14,656 ms to 229 ms; his FPS row is not a clean A/B (his words). His visible
side effect: a one-frame white flash on the player the first time he stepped into water. Our port
is smaller: no settings, no UI, no test file; an env switch instead, and our CanSkipDraw rule
decides which draws may be skipped (his CanDiscardPendingDraw kept native draws; in our tree 0508
already skips native draws while they translate, so the skip path is known to work).

Off: one live-switch load per draw whose pipeline is not built yet (built pipelines return before
it). notify_all instead of notify_one once per pipeline build. Changes no shader translation, and
the switch is not in the stamp's switch list (the name is only in common/live_switch.h). It does
change the source-stamp translation id: the build makes a derived copy of vk_pipeline_cache.cpp
(vk_pipeline_cache_cost.cpp) that is in the translation id's file list, so with
EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP_SOURCE_STAMP=1 a build with this patch does not reuse an older build's kept
store (corrected in T160 after the T156 review; the first text said the id stays).

Risk when on: a draw missing for a frame or a few (a flash, a missing object, a missing effect)
while its pipeline builds. A draw that writes something the game reads later (a render to texture
that is not cleared each frame) could leave it stale for longer. The budget is spent on the GPU
thread, so up to the budget a frame is added to frames that build; a budget of 100 ms would undo
the point.

What a test should look at: the same cold-ish route (house -> door -> village, first sight of the
area past the field), on vs off, or live A/B with EDEN_UP_LIVE_SWITCHES=1. Look at the longest
frames and frames over 100 ms at first sights, the "worker: waiting for a pipeline build" hang
site if the hang watch logs it, and the new line every 10 s: "Bounded build wait (0751) in the
last 10 s: X ms waited, N draws got their pipeline in time, M skipped (budget B ms a frame)". M is
how many draws were missing for a frame. Picture: record and look for one-frame flashes or
missing pieces at first sights (reel.py side by side). Also try 4 and 16 if 8 helps.
